For $n >= 2$, $a$ is a unit modulo $n$ if and only if $gcd(a, n) = 1$:
$ gcd(a, n) = 1 &<=> a x + n y = 1 quad "for some" x, y in ZZ \
               &<=> a x equiv 1 mod n \
               &<=> a "is a unit modulo" n. $
In particular, when $p$ is prime every $a equiv.not 0 mod p$ satisfies $gcd(a, p) = 1$, so every non-zero residue is a unit modulo $p$.
